  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Thursday: Another day of warmer than average \ntemperatures with dry conditions is ahead of us due to the \npersistent ridge over much of the western US. High temperatures \ntoday will be in the mid to high 70s, around 10 degrees above normal \nfor this time of year. Clear skies and light winds will promote \nefficient radiational cooling, keeping overnight lows closer to \nnormal. Northern sheltered valleys will see temperatures in the high \n30s, while temperatures in the Columbia Basin, Spokane, and Palouse \nareas will see lows in the mid 40s. High temperatures will warm by a \ndegree or two each day through Thursday as the ridge restrengthens. \nEnjoy these warmer fall days while they last, because a cooldown is \nin order for the weekend. \n\nFriday through Sunday: Cluster analysis roughly 80 percent of \nensemble members favoring a strong trough and associated cold front \nmoving through the area Friday afternoon. There remains considerable \nuncertainty in the track, timing, and strength of the trough. \nDespite these differences, guidance remains consistent on a sharp \ndrop in temperatures from Friday into Saturday, along with \nincreasing winds. Highs will drop 10 or more degrees, with \ntemperatures returning to near normal levels in the upper 50s to low \n60s. Precipitation chances remain highest in the mountains, though \nsnow levels do fall to around 4000-5000 feet in the central \nPanhandle, bringing a small chance for mountain snow. Elsewhere, \nprecipitation chances reach 15 to 20 percent. At this point, winds \nappear to be the primary impact. On Friday afternoon, wind gusts in \nthe Waterville Plateau and Columbia Basin look to increase to 30 to \n35 mph, with gusts 20 to 25 mph elsewhere. Given the prolonged dry \nstretch, blowing dust will be possible across the Columbia Basin \nwhere soils remain dry. These gusts will create difficult travel \nconditions, especially for high profile vehicles, and could blow \naround unsecured objects.  \n\nGusty conditions will continue Saturday and Sunday as winds from the \nnorth increase. On Saturday, gusty winds will be mostly confined to \nthe Okanogan Valley, Waterville Plateau, and basin areas. On Sunday, \ngusty winds will be more widespread, again reaching 30 to 35 mph in \nthe basin and Waterville Plateau. Bottom line, expect a potentially \nchilly and windy weekend in the Inland Northwest, and stay tuned as \nthe forecast becomes clearer in the coming days. /AS",
